{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"dc45afce59212e1a4409883292816a1d2c80debf77ca772c4a15377c597faf02","slug":"pages.m05-linear-relationships-1-of-8","location":"/pages/052-m05-linear-relationships-1-of-8.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Linear Relationships (1 of 8)","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","free":true,"CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/textbook/build/linear1-891806c83076a6cc7d4365589606457d.gif","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"052-m05-linear-relationships-1-of-8.md","url":"/textbook/build/052-m05-linear-relat-80bbdc4d497f817c0ec1fc9ffd4076d4.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"dAT5lutlJM"}],"key":"DJlljGwJrS"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"- Interpret the value of the correlation coefficient, and be aware of its limitations as a numerical measure of the association between two quantitative variables.","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"DVLs1lTTNj"}],"key":"vLQFZ3MA92"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"eVrPBiww0R"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"key":"s1VzZNF6bq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So far we have visualized relationships between two quantitative variables using scatterplots, and described the overall pattern of a relationship by considering its direction, form, and strength. We noted that assessing the strength of a relationship just by looking at the scatterplot is quite difficult, and therefore we need to supplement the scatterplot with some kind of numerical measure that will help us assess the strength.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"tXM9HzCrqd"}],"key":"IgTIO9LFEz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this part, we will restrict our attention to the ","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"ITMzCHkXWc"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"special case of relationships that have a linear form","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"R0mLcNbYKS"}],"key":"hJCJBJghmo"},{"type":"text","value":", since they are quite common and relatively simple to detect. More importantly, there exists a numerical measure that assesses the strength of the linear relationship between two quantitative variables with which we can supplement the scatterplot. We will introduce this numerical measure here and discuss it in detail.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"G8V3eqyX31"}],"key":"e9WN1J8S74"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even though from this point on we are going to focus only on linear relationships, it is important to remember that ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"pYnh175ie8"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"not every relationship between two quantitative variables has a linear form.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"kUJ5w0jW0o"}],"key":"dfbDqGPuwF"},{"type":"text","value":" We have actually seen several examples of relationships that are not linear. The statistical tools that will be introduced here are ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"lftVFA1jnk"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"appropriate only for examining linear relationships,","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"K2X6r6s2e0"}],"key":"b75bXYYUiK"},{"type":"text","value":" and as we will see, when they are used in nonlinear situations, these tools can lead to errors in reasoning.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"rAVSHNKJ5q"}],"key":"XyT6gegOwz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s start with a motivating example. Consider the following two scatterplots.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"SymIpRDrlv"}],"key":"Y29cN3haw4"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/linear1-891806c83076a6cc7d4365589606457d.gif","alt":"Two scatterplots, displaying the same data. However, the first scatterplot has a much larger scale on its axes than the second. Because of this, the first scatterplot has its data points clustered closer together than in the second scatterplot. Both have their points arranged roughly in a linear manner. In addition, the second scatterplot appears to have some outliers. All this is merely due to the scale change.","key":"ZHmPiKAlss","urlSource":"images/linear1.gif"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"iArTgytz9i"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can see that in both cases, the direction of the relationship is ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"fQzIAcoozB"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"positive","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"mz10WX1N8O"}],"key":"VvysC555O1"},{"type":"text","value":" and the form of the relationship is ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"p6gEMrli4q"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"linear","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"a6fAdEfZC5"}],"key":"TpdXbKAfbJ"},{"type":"text","value":". What about the strength? Recall that the strength of a relationship is the extent to which the data follow its form.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"o623bLDI5Z"}],"key":"xUsVqr106J"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"ODdoZPE6vt"}],"key":"RHlbXtmQAI"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Learn By Doing**\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"KlYCEb5VUR"}],"key":"S5VMnOHtaQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The purpose of this example was to illustrate how assessing the strength of the linear relationship from a scatterplot alone is problematic, since our judgment might be affected by the scale on which the values are plotted. This example, therefore, provides a motivation for the ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"ZwzwZ9IafP"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"need","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Kv3tzOLC8e"}],"key":"tdOD0GbGQi"},{"type":"text","value":"to supplement the scatterplot with a ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"tWv1mJxtbp"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"numerical measure","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"OtDYxCGZ50"}],"key":"zsy8ArKBo8"},{"type":"text","value":" that will ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"rGpigqDL9t"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"measure the strength","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"CXu3Q4Po4L"}],"key":"H3oXzkwiIJ"},{"type":"text","value":" of the linear relationship between two quantitative variables.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"EPMUTB3e4w"}],"key":"ikzzZw8bAY"}],"key":"vxnbQKxS68"}],"key":"SBpuoAuZoZ"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Scatterplot (5 of 5)","url":"/pages/m05-scatterplot-5-of-5","group":"Module 5: Examining Relationships"},"next":{"title":"Linear Relationships (2 of 8)","url":"/pages/m05-linear-relationships-2-of-8","group":"Module 5: Examining Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}